  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Nature article is still in an early impact phase, but it is already showing signs of scholarly reuse: OpenAlex records 3 citations and 4 downstream reuse instances. The citing record is small and spans an editorial, a journal article, and a preprint, suggesting that the piece is being drawn into adjacent discussions about science communication rather than accumulating broad, mature citation volume.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal in the record is not volume but reach across formats. The article has been cited by three 2026 works in different publication types, including Frontiers in Communication, an editorial in Immunopharmacology and Immunotoxicology, and a medRxiv preprint. That pattern points to early uptake in communication-focused and interdisciplinary STEM conversations, especially around how science is translated for public audiences.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The topical footprint of the citing literature is also consistent with that interpretation. The downstream works connect the article to themes such as climate change communication, participatory methods, dissemination, and social media science communication, with additional links into healthcare and education contexts. In other words, the article appears to be functioning as a conceptual reference point for work on how scientific ideas move beyond the page and into public-facing channels.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond citations, the reuse graph records four forms of documented reuse, and web mentions show light but real discussion in a small number of online sources, including a Substack newsletter and two blogs. Those mentions add context for visibility, though they remain modest and should be read as limited public attention rather than broad dissemination. The article is also listed in OpenAIRE as a closed-access journal article, and Crossref data-citation status indicates that dataset citation tracking completed successfully.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record suggests an article whose impact is still emerging and whose influence is currently concentrated in science communication and adjacent applied fields. The evidence supports early scholarly uptake and some online circulation, but the citation base remains small, so the most defensible interpretation is that this work is being noticed and reused in conversations about how science should be communicated, not yet widely embedded across the literature.</p>",
